Of course, I've not really been playing for the last 11 years, so it's
not quite so dramatic as it sounds. I just started playing Nethack
again a few weeks ago. But it's a good headline.

The last time I ascended was in 1997 on Nethack 3.1.3 as Kid Jane the
Valkyrie. Here's that YAAP post, courtesy of the Google Groups
archive: http://bit.ly/5BoOsZ

This time around, I ascended with Iolo, the male human wizard. What
fun! A very different experience from playing as a Valk.

Iolo's starting equipment was mixed. Big whoop for the ring of
adornment, but the ring of polymorph control showed promise. A scroll
of enchant armor brought the cloak of magic resistance up to +1 and I
honestly can't remember what the other two scrolls were. Maybe one was
light?

The first two levels posed no problems, but I worried quite a bit
about level 3. Many games back, a more unfortunate wizard had the
pleasure of seeing the inside of a purple worm that he met in a
darkened hallway -- the product of a polymorph trap, I assume. That
bones file has killed two other characters since (I play on my own
desktop). Luckily, Iolo did not add his bones to the Purple Worm
file.

I ventured down into the mines to have a look-see -- if they're lit, I
like to check out the shops in Mine Town -- but the first level was,
alas, dark, and I've had very bad experiences trudging around the
darkened mines too early.

So Iolo headed on down to Sokoban, which is a great addition to
Nethack. On the way the RNG smiled on me. I found an armor shop on
level 5 where I was able to buy a pair of speed boots and, deeper
down, on the same level ast he Sokoban stairwell, I discovered an
antique bookshop. Hello Identify, Cure Sickness and Magic Missile
(plus several other spells)

Naturally, I screwed up in Sokoban and had to force bolt two boulders
and read a scroll of earth, so my luck was somewhat hosed. However, I
did manage to find a wand of polymorph among the boulders, which made
it possible to safely clear out the treasure zoo.

Poof, I'm a silver dragon! This was fun.

After the battle was over, I got hungry and said to myself, "No
problem, I'll just chow down on one of these delicious, freshly killed
corpses!" Alas, either I had waited too long, or that gnome had been a
zombie before I blasted him, but I immediately found myself deathly
ill with no holy water or extra healing potions. I prayed to Thoth. He
was annoyed and no help at all. I tried casting "Cure sickness" a few
times, but my 75% failure rate was too high for the RNG. Crap. I was
staring YASD in the face.

After calming down a bit, I realized that I still had some charges in
my wand, so, poof!, I'm a ghoul. Sickness averted, I hung around until
I felt a bit more like myself, grabbed the amulet of reflection and
headed back up to the mines.

I still had no light source, but I did have wands of light, so I took
it upon myself to illuminate the upper floors. When I reached Mine
Town, what a bonanza! I I found a bevy of spellbooks and other gear
from a previous incarnation of Iolo. Casting identify to reveal my
inventory showed I was the proud owner of a cursed magic lamp, thanks
to the bones, and also showed that the bag I'd found on the floor of
the mines wasn't just a sack, as I'd thought, but a bone fide bag of
holding.

The icing on the cake? A co-aligned temple. I tried to sacrifice for
Magicbane with whatever hapless gnomes and dwarves wandered by, but
only got a four-leafed clover. Which, considering my experience in
Sokoban, was probably a good thing.

After a quick trip back upstairs to the Oracle to dilute some junk
potions in her fountains, I made holy water at the temple and blessed
the BoH and the Magic Lamp. One rub later, and I'm wearing +2 SDSM. It
was time to head downstairs to Mine's End, where I grabbed the
luckstone and maxed out my dagger skill. I'd collected a pile of about
20 uncursed daggers, so I enchanted them up to +2 with the scrolls I'd
been hoarding. Expert skill with a big pile of enchanted daggers is a
pretty awesome sight to behold.

On the way down, I'd picked up a couple of wands of create monster, so
when I returned to Mine Town, I finally convinced Thoth to give me
Magicbane -- I had to exhaust both wands! It was at this point that I
thought I that I might be looking at my second ascension.

Heading deeper into the dungeon, I met another ascension worthy
character's bones who suffered YASD. In that case, while I was
traveling around the dungeon wearing a towel, I carefully killed a
cockatrice from a distance and then walked over the corpse without
realizing I'd forgotten to put on my gloves after BUC testing them at
the altar.

It was an awful experience then, but this old character's sorrow was
my new one's joy, as there was a ring of levitation and gauntlets of
dexterity in the pile. Plus, much to my shock, his pet black dragon
was still tame (alas, I lost him in Gehenom somewhere)! The bones also
contained Mjollnir, which was never put to any use but was lugged
around anyways to make sure nothing else ever did.

Medusa's Island did notpossess a Titan, and so was a snap, as was the
Castle. I'd inherited a wand of death from a werejackal in the Big
Room who'd not noticed my reflective armor (or my CoMR for that
matter), so anytime things got hairy in the castle (and they only did
once when I found myself hemmed in by a master mind flayer) I zapped
it. I figured I'd have finger of death soon enough.

The WoW had 2 charges, which I burned for my first magic marker and a
helm of brilliance. A bit of polypiling gave me a second magic marker,
and so, pretty soon, I had fully enchanted weapons and an AC of -32. I
never did find Ludios, but really didn't have to as there were a
couple of leprechaun halls and several treasure zoos. My natural AC
once I entered Gehenom was about 1 or so.

I found a potion of gain level, which sent me to exp. 14, at which
point I made a beeline for the quest, which was a snap. I even found a
magic lamp on the first level, but that djinni, alas, was an ingrate.
The Eye of the Aethiopica and the Bell in hand, I recharged my wand
and burned a wish for a blessed spellbook of magic mapping, which was
a godsend in Gehenom. Don't leave the Valley of the Dead without it.

Now it was time to get crowned so I could get my hands on Finger of
Death, and with the Create Monster spell, it wasn't hard at all. Plus,
it gave me shock resistance, which I was missing. Downward into
Gehenom.

My second big scare of the game was in Jubilex' swamp because, the
instant he teleported to me, he summoned Demogorgon, who immediately
sickened me. I cured myself with a blessed unicorn horn and dug a hole
through the floor, but, dammit, this was no teleport as well!
(Asmodeus' lair) Luckily, though, Demogorgon ended up on the other
side of a wall, so I invoked the eye to return to my Sokoban stash,
where I was promptly engulfed by Jubilex. A zap with a WoD and a bonk
on the head (or pseudopod, I suppose) and he was vanquished. But what
to do about Demogorgon? The answer, it turns out, was a partly eaten
chickatrice corpse. Just one wish left, alas.

Liches, it seemed, were everywhere, but with Magicbane eating their
curses and an AC of -38, they were mostly an annoyance. Abut half the
time, I could throw a half dozen +6 daggers at a Master Lich and it
would go down without having a chance to teleport to the upstair. What
really frightened me were aquatic monsters with a drowning attack like
Kraken or Electric Eels, especially since I never found nor was able
to polypile an oilskin cloak. In the end, though, I made sure to be
very careful around water and killed them from afar with magic missile
or finger of death.

Master mind flayers likewise got the finger of death treatment. Only
once did one of them manage to get their tentacles on my head, but it
was luckily foiled by the thick layer of grease on my helmet. FoD for
you, Mr. Brain Sucker.

The other demon princes posed no problems, and Vlad and Rodney were
never a hassle. The Eye of Aethiopica plus Finger of Death =3D Nearly
unstoppble.

The High Priest of Moloch was wearing a CoMR, so Finger of Death
didn't do the trick. Luckily, my pile of enchanted daggers did.

On the way back up, the mysterious force was a pain in the neck, but I
eventually found my way to the castle and methodically climbed back up
to my stash at Sokoban. I wrote 4 scrolls of gold detection, a couple
of blessed scrolls of charging and a bless scroll of genocide in case
I decided I needed it on the plane of water. The RNG was kind enough
to send a purple worm and a Frost Giant to Sokoban, which I charmed,
thinking they might come in handy. Rodney, too, paid a visit and I
welcomed him warmly with my FoD.

The planes themselves were not a problem, except at the end of the
plane of air. My Frost Giant got whacked by an air elemental and I'd
forgotten to remove my RoC before summoning my remaining pet with a
magic whistle. The poor thing engulfed me and I didn't have any more
wands of digging, having jettisoned them to rid myself of weight after
the Plane of Earth. FoD and a rumble of thunder later, I headed to the
astral plane.

I don't know how I screwed this up AGAIN, but just as I did with my
Valkyrie, I mistook my temple for someone else's. I SWEAR I saw the
high priest ID'd as a priest of Ptah, but that must have been an
ordinary priest, not a high priest. So I visited ... not 2 ... not
three ... not even four, but FIVE temples. I started right, killed
Pestilance, and mistook my temple for Ptahs. So, I trudged back across
to the center and easily (or so I thought) ID'd that temple by
mistake. An errant FoD hit the high priest of Ahnur. Yikes!

On my way out of the middle temple, I got cornered by Death, which was
a scary experience. I had an AoLS on, plus two more in my pack, but
still -- in just a couple of turns, my max HP went from 165 to 97 and
my actual HP was at 36. Luckily, an angel obliged me and took him out,
so I scooted by. At this point, I burned my last wish for a second
partly eaten chickatrice corpse and made my way quickly to the
lefthand temple where I discovered, much to my horror, that the third
temple was Ptah's.

So, I trudged back to the middle temple, determined to chat directly
with the Priest to make sure it wasn't Thoth's, and zapped Famine a
couple of times with the FoD on the way over. Avoided Death and got
next to the Priest of Anhur (auuuggggghhhh!) who said, "Why would I
want to talk to you?" and then proceeded to open prodigious wounds all
over my body. I beat a hasty retreat through the tumult, avoided Death
for a third time and hacked my way back to the first temple, which was
Thoth's, where I finally met my reward.

